This iterator does not keep a stack or an inorder array. It creates a temporary link from the rightmost node of a left subtree back to its parent. That link is removed when the parent is reached, so the tree is restored as iteration continues.

The iterator changes pointers temporarily. If iteration stops halfway, some temporary links can still be present, so this method is less convenient when the tree must stay untouched at every moment.

```javascript
class BSTIterator {
    constructor(root) {
        this.current = root;
    }

    hasNext() {
        return this.current !== null;
    }

    next() {
        while (this.current !== null) {
            if (this.current.left === null) {
                const answer = this.current.data;
                this.current = this.current.right;
                return answer;
            }

            let predecessor = this.current.left;
            while (
                predecessor.right !== null &&
                predecessor.right !== this.current
            ) {
                predecessor = predecessor.right;
            }

            if (predecessor.right === null) {
                predecessor.right = this.current;
                this.current = this.current.left;
            } else {
                predecessor.right = null;
                const answer = this.current.data;
                this.current = this.current.right;
                return answer;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

For an empty tree, `hasNext()` is false and `next()` returns `-1`. Each temporary link is created and removed once, so the total time is `O(n)` and the extra space is `O(1)`.

Only the left path is stored at the start. When a node is returned, its right child supplies the next left path. This avoids making a list of every value.

A quick dry run for a tree rooted at 4 with left child 2 and right child 6 starts with `[4, 2]` in the stack. The values come out as 2, 4, and then 6.

```python
class BSTIterator:
    def __init__(self, root):
        self.stack = []
        self._push_left(root)

    def _push_left(self, node):
        while node is not None:
            self.stack.append(node)
            node = node.left

    def hasNext(self):
        return len(self.stack) > 0

    def next(self):
        node = self.stack.pop()
        self._push_left(node.right)
        return node.data
```

The important edge cases are a null root and a leaf node. Both are handled by the empty stack checks. Every node is processed once, giving `O(n)` total time and `O(h)` extra space.

A stack keeps the path to the next smallest node. The constructor pushes the left side of the tree, and `next()` moves into the right subtree after removing one node.

For example, on a tree with values 2, 1, and 3, the first stack top is 1. After returning it, node 2 is added, then 3 follows. The tree itself is not changed.

```cpp
#include <stack>
using namespace std;

class BSTIterator {
private:
    stack<TreeNode*> nodes;

    void pushLeft(TreeNode* node) {
        while (node != nullptr) {
            nodes.push(node);
            node = node->left;
        }
    }

public:
    BSTIterator(TreeNode* root) {
        pushLeft(root);
    }

    bool hasNext() {
        return !nodes.empty();
    }

    int next() {
        TreeNode* node = nodes.top();
        nodes.pop();
        pushLeft(node->right);
        return node->data;
    }
};
```

Each node is pushed and removed once, so the total time is `O(n)`, or `O(1)` amortized per call. The stack uses `O(h)` space, where `h` is the tree height.

This version builds the complete inorder sequence before any calls are made. Inorder traversal of a binary search tree gives sorted values, so `next()` only needs to read the current list position.

It uses more memory than a lazy iterator, but the code is easy to check. An empty tree simply creates an empty list, and `hasNext()` returns false.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

class BSTIterator {
    private final List<Integer> values = new ArrayList<>();
    private int index = 0;

    public BSTIterator(TreeNode root) {
        addInorder(root);
    }

    private void addInorder(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }
        addInorder(node.left);
        values.add(node.data);
        addInorder(node.right);
    }

    public boolean hasNext() {
        return index < values.size();
    }

    public int next() {
        return values.get(index++);
    }
}
```

The constructor takes `O(n)` time and the calls together take `O(n)` time. The list and recursion use `O(n)` space.

The constructor stores the left spine in a stack, just like walking to the smallest value first. The stack always contains the next possible nodes, while `Next()` adds the left spine of the returned node's right subtree.

The input tree is not modified. A single-node tree is also fine: it is pushed once, returned once, and then the stack becomes empty.

```csharp
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class BSTIterator {
    private readonly Stack<TreeNode> stack = new Stack<TreeNode>();

    public BSTIterator(TreeNode root) {
        PushLeft(root);
    }

    private void PushLeft(TreeNode node) {
        while (node != null) {
            stack.Push(node);
            node = node.left;
        }
    }

    public int Next() {
        TreeNode node = stack.Pop();
        PushLeft(node.right);
        return node.val;
    }

    public bool HasNext() {
        return stack.Count > 0;
    }
}
```

The stack needs `O(h)` space. Across all calls, nodes are pushed and popped once, so the total running time is `O(n)`. Calling `HasNext()` does not move the iterator.

